---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import ContentSection from '../components/ContentSection.astro';
import Callout from '../components/Callout.astro';
import LinkCard from '../components/LinkCard.astro';

const relatedPages = [
  { title: 'Glossary', href: '/glossary', description: 'Complete betting terminology' },
  { title: 'Line Movement', href: '/understanding-line-movement', description: 'Why and how odds change' },
  { title: 'Odds Converter', href: '/tools/odds-converter', description: 'Convert between odds formats' },
];
---

<Layout
  title="How Betting Odds Work"
  description="Learn how to read and understand betting odds including moneylines, point spreads, totals, implied probability, and the vig in plain language."
>
  <Hero
    title="How Betting Odds Work"
    subtitle="Understanding Moneylines, Spreads, Totals, and Implied Probability"
    size="medium"
  />

  <div class="container content-container">
    <article class="page-content">
      <ContentSection>
        <div class="quick-take">
          <h2>Quick Take</h2>
          <p>
            Betting odds tell you two things: how much you can win and what probability the market assigns to each outcome. This guide explains how to read American odds, point spreads, and totals, and how to calculate implied probability. Understanding odds is the foundation of informed betting—without it, you can't evaluate whether a bet offers value.
          </p>
        </div>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="What Are Betting Odds?" id="definition">
        <p>
          Betting odds are prices set by sportsbooks that determine how much you can win on a bet. They also imply a probability for each outcome. Learning to interpret odds in both ways—as payouts and as probabilities—is essential for evaluating bets.
        </p>
        <p>
          Odds exist because people disagree about the future. Sportsbooks set prices based on their models and adjust them based on betting action. Bettors try to find spots where they believe the true probability differs from what the odds suggest.
        </p>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="How Do American Odds Work?" id="american-odds">
        <p>
          American odds are the standard format in the United States. They're expressed as positive or negative numbers relative to $100.
        </p>

        <h3>What do negative odds mean?</h3>
        <p>
          Negative odds indicate the favorite. The number shows how much you need to risk to win $100. For example, -150 means you must bet $150 to win $100 in profit. If your bet wins, you get back $250 total ($150 stake + $100 profit).
        </p>
        <p>
          The larger the negative number, the bigger the favorite. A -300 favorite is more heavily favored than a -150 favorite.
        </p>

        <h3>What do positive odds mean?</h3>
        <p>
          Positive odds indicate the underdog. The number shows how much you'd win on a $100 bet. For example, +130 means a $100 bet returns $130 in profit. If your bet wins, you get back $230 total ($100 stake + $130 profit).
        </p>
        <p>
          The larger the positive number, the bigger the underdog. A +500 underdog is less likely to win than a +130 underdog according to the market.
        </p>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="What Is a Point Spread?" id="spread">
        <p>
          A point spread is a handicap applied to the favorite to create a more balanced betting market. Instead of betting on who wins, you're betting on whether a team will win by more than a certain margin (or lose by less than that margin).
        </p>

        <h3>How do spreads work?</h3>
        <p>
          If a team is favored by 7 points (-7), they must win by more than 7 for spread bets on them to win. If they win by exactly 7, the bet is a "push" and stakes are refunded. If they win by 6 or less (or lose), spread bets on them lose.
        </p>
        <p>
          The underdog at +7 wins the spread if they win outright or lose by fewer than 7 points.
        </p>

        <h3>Why do spreads have half-points?</h3>
        <p>
          Half-points (like -7.5 or +3.5) eliminate the possibility of a push. They're called "hooks." A team at -7.5 must win by 8 or more. There's no way to tie.
        </p>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="What Are Totals (Over/Unders)?" id="totals">
        <p>
          A total is a bet on the combined score of both teams. The sportsbook sets a number, and you bet whether the actual combined score will be over or under that number.
        </p>

        <h3>When do totals matter?</h3>
        <p>
          Totals are useful when you have an opinion on game flow but not on who wins. If you expect a high-scoring game but aren't sure which team will win, betting the over lets you act on that view.
        </p>
        <p>
          Weather, injuries, pace of play, and defensive matchups all influence totals.
        </p>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="How Do I Calculate Implied Probability?" id="implied-probability">
        <p>
          Implied probability converts odds into a percentage, showing what chance the market assigns to each outcome.
        </p>

        <h3>Formula for negative odds</h3>
        <p>
          Implied probability = |Odds| / (|Odds| + 100)
        </p>
        <p>
          Example: -150 odds imply 150 / (150 + 100) = 150 / 250 = 60% probability.
        </p>

        <h3>Formula for positive odds</h3>
        <p>
          Implied probability = 100 / (Odds + 100)
        </p>
        <p>
          Example: +130 odds imply 100 / (130 + 100) = 100 / 230 = 43.5% probability.
        </p>

        <Callout type="info" title="The Probabilities Don't Add Up to 100%">
          <p>
            If you calculate implied probability for both sides of a bet, they'll total more than 100%. This excess is the vig—the sportsbook's built-in profit margin.
          </p>
        </Callout>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="What Is the Vig (Juice)?" id="vig">
        <p>
          The vig (short for vigorish, also called "juice") is the sportsbook's commission. It's built into the odds and ensures the book profits regardless of the outcome.
        </p>

        <h3>How does the vig work?</h3>
        <p>
          Standard spread and total bets are priced at -110 on each side. This means you risk $110 to win $100. If one bettor wins and one loses, the book collects $110 from the loser, pays $100 to the winner, and keeps $10.
        </p>

        <h3>Why does the vig matter?</h3>
        <p>
          The vig sets your break-even rate. At -110, you need to win 52.4% of your bets just to break even (110 / 210 = 52.38%). This is the hurdle you must clear before making any profit.
        </p>
        <p>
          Lower-vig sportsbooks (offering -105 or even -102) reduce this hurdle. Over hundreds of bets, the difference compounds significantly.
        </p>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="What This Means for Bettors" id="what-means">
        <p>
          Understanding odds transforms how you think about betting:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Every bet has an implied probability:</strong> Before betting, convert the odds to see what the market thinks.</li>
          <li><strong>Value comes from disagreement:</strong> If you believe a team has a 55% chance to cover but the odds imply 48%, that's potential value.</li>
          <li><strong>The vig is your cost:</strong> Factor it into every decision. It's the price of playing.</li>
          <li><strong>Small differences matter:</strong> Getting +115 instead of +105 on the same bet improves your return significantly over time.</li>
        </ul>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="Common Misconceptions" id="misconceptions">
        <h3>"Favorites always cover"</h3>
        <p>
          Favorites are favorites because they're more likely to win outright. But against the spread, the line is set to make both sides approximately equal. Favorites cover about 50% of the time over large samples.
        </p>

        <h3>"Big underdogs never win"</h3>
        <p>
          Underdogs win outright far more often than their odds suggest to most casual observers. A +300 underdog wins roughly 20-25% of the time. That's not rare.
        </p>

        <h3>"The vig doesn't matter for casual betting"</h3>
        <p>
          The vig matters for everyone. It's the reason most bettors lose over time. Even if you're betting for fun, understanding that you're paying a commission on every bet is important.
        </p>

        <h3>"Odds predict what will happen"</h3>
        <p>
          Odds reflect market consensus, not certainty. They're influenced by betting action, public perception, and sportsbook risk management—not just objective probability.
        </p>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="When This Matters Most" id="when-matters">
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Before every bet:</strong> Always convert odds to implied probability and compare to your estimate.</li>
          <li><strong>When line shopping:</strong> Small odds differences create meaningful value over time.</li>
          <li><strong>During live betting:</strong> Odds change rapidly; understanding them helps you react quickly.</li>
          <li><strong>When evaluating promotions:</strong> "Boosted odds" only matter if they actually offer better value than standard lines.</li>
        </ul>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="Key Takeaways" id="takeaways">
        <ul>
          <li>American odds show potential profit relative to $100 bets</li>
          <li>Negative odds = favorites; positive odds = underdogs</li>
          <li>Point spreads handicap the favorite to balance the market</li>
          <li>Totals let you bet on combined score without picking a winner</li>
          <li>Implied probability reveals what chance the market assigns to each outcome</li>
          <li>The vig is your cost of participation—it sets your break-even rate</li>
          <li>Understanding odds is essential for identifying value</li>
        </ul>
      </ContentSection>

      <ContentSection title="Related Resources" id="related">
        <div class="related-grid">
          {relatedPages.map((page) => (
            <LinkCard
              title={page.title}
              href={page.href}
              description={page.description}
            />
          ))}
        </div>
      </ContentSection>
    </article>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .page-content {
    padding: var(--spacing-3xl) 0;
  }

  .quick-take {
    background-color: var(--color-surface);
    border-left: 4px solid var(--color-accent);
    padding: var(--spacing-lg);
    border-radius: 0 var(--border-radius) var(--border-radius) 0;
    margin-bottom: var(--spacing-xl);
  }

  .quick-take h2 {
    font-size: 1rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    color: var(--color-accent);
    margin-bottom: var(--spacing-sm);
  }

  .quick-take p {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
  }

  .related-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    gap: var(--spacing-md);
    margin-top: var(--spacing-lg);
  }

  @media (max-width: 768px) {
    .related-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
